gladiadores.insert_last("Wuula:0:200:30000:Wuula is a black man of about 1.70 meters, a native of Numidia in North Africa.  His build is lean yet powerful, and his physique is that of a fighter of great agility.  He bears a large diagonal scar across his torso, most likely the result of a clash with one of the defeated fighters on his list.  Wuula, when he was free, was a hunter.  Today he is a gladiator trained in the art of retiarius combat, wielding the trident and the casting net.  This gladiator was captured by the Roman legion at a salt mine in North Africa and brought here to be a slave.  However, Caius Felix, lanista to Julius Caesar, saw in his physique a fine prospect for fighting in the arena and bought him for the games.  Wuula was then trained by the finest retiarii of the school of Caius Felix, and today he is a respected retiarius.:Soldier");
gladiadores.insert_last("Wazax:0:200:60000:Wazax is what one might call a typical spoil of war.  During the conquest of Greece by the Roman Empire, many children were captured and made slaves for the pleasure of the Roman nobility.  With Wazax it was no different.  During the conquest of Troy, Wazax was captured and taken to Rome to become a slave.  However, his skill with spears, drawn from his Trojan upbringing, proved useful when he seized a legionary's pilum to defend himself from the overseer, which cost him a severe beating and earned him the admiration of Lucius Proximus, a renowned lanista of Rome.  From then on he was raised to be a hoplomachus gladiator, a discipline that consists of the art of fighting with the spear.  However much glory Wazax has won in the arena, he does not hide his origins as a slave.  His back and legs are covered in whip marks.  His nickname is \"Tiger\", because of the whip scars, which look like the animal's stripes.:Soldier");
gladiadores.insert_last("Cairis:201:1000:90000:Cairis is a typical Gaul.  Red-haired, with long hair, a thick moustache and a long beard arranged in three braids.  And a fine physique, though packed into little more than 1.60m, which makes him the shortest of all the gladiators.  But make no mistake; Cairis is very strong.  To be captured, he almost wiped out an entire decuria single-handedly!  Cairis was captured on the orders of Marcus Gracchus, a former general of the Gallic campaign and today a lanista of Rome.  During a skirmish, Gracchus saw the brilliance of this warrior and decided to capture him as a gift for Caesar, a prize from the campaign in Gaul.  Caesar then ordered Gracchus to train the Gaul for the arena.  And today Cairis earns his living in the combats of the Colosseum.:Mercenary");
gladiadores.insert_last("Claudius:201:1000:120000:Claudius was tied to the arena from an early age.  Born into a very poor family, he began working in the barracks of the Colosseum as a stable cleaner.  He learned the craft of the forge and soon became the blacksmith of the Colosseum, responsible for maintaining the chariots, the horseshoes and the equipment used by the gladiators.  In time he took up the office of executioner, charged with cutting off the heads of prisoners in the Colosseum for the delight of the crowd.  This practice gave Claudius a taste for slaughter, and he soon entered a tryout for the training of new gladiators.  Claudius carried out a true massacre at that tryout and was, naturally, the winner.  He was then taken in by Caius Felix and became a well-known gladiator.  He is famous for collecting the skullcaps of those he defeats.:Mercenary");
gladiadores.insert_last("Lucius:1001:2000:150000:Lucius is an enormous gladiator from the region of Thrace, north of Greece, a region famous for the savagery of its warriors.  Lucius is no different.  He was recruited young into the hordes of Thracian barbarians who frequently raided the Roman provinces of northern Greece, until he was finally captured.  The centurion of one of the fortified camps saw that Lucius was no more than a boy of little over 13 years.  Yet he had almost the height and build of a regular legionary of the legion.  He decided to give this prisoner special treatment and to train him for the local arenas.  Lucius did so well in the arenas that he was later bought for his weight in gold by Gaius, a famous lanista of Rome.  Since then he has been one of the champions of the Roman arena, known and renowned for his cruelty and his endurance of wounds.:President");
gladiadores.insert_last("Sirix:1001:2000:180000:Sirix is a gladiator of barbarian, eastern origin, most likely a Hun.  As uncertain as his origin is, so too is the path that led him to become a gladiator.  But it is known that he is the cruelest and most sadistic gladiator of all those in the Colosseum.  His pleasure is to disarm his enemy so as to slaughter him little by little, exulting in every spurt of blood.  He is able to fight in this sadistic manner thanks to his enormous and well-known agility, which affords him an almost acrobatic method of combat.  Sirix has another peculiarity.  He fights without heavy armor, baring his body, which is covered in tribal tattoos.:President");
gladiadores.insert_last("Cressus:2001:3000:210000:Cressus comes from a family with a long Roman tradition in the gladiator barracks.  The son of a gladiator, Cressus learned the daily life of the barracks from a very early age.  He was his father's squire, and even watched him be killed in the arena.  His fury was so great that he took the sword from his father's still-warm hand and hacked his killer to pieces with tremendous ferocity.  That was enough for him to be hailed as \"the sensation\" of that year's games.  Ever since, Cressus has devoted his life to killing any and every warrior who crosses his path.:Dictator");
gladiadores.insert_last("Duron:2001:3000:240000:Duron is a slender gladiator with a very agile look about him.  He was trained by his brother Argon, who held him in high esteem, before Argon's death during the revolts for the liberation of the slaves led by Spartacus.  His agility and his strength, combined with his intelligence in the fight, make Duron a terrible opponent.:Dictator");
gladiadores.insert_last("Aiellus:3001:4000:270000:Aiellus was once a Roman legionary.  However, his cruelty and lack of military discipline led to his expulsion from the legion.  He thus became a deserter, shunned throughout the Roman world.  Yet he saw in the Colosseum the chance to gain safe passage across the whole Roman world, as well as to be paid and well treated for doing what he most loved to do.  Since entering the barracks of the Colosseum, his life has changed for the better.  He gained fame, money and reputation.  And in exchange for all this he lost only an eye, in one of his fights.  Aiellus is easily recognized by the black cloth eyepatch he wears, made from a silk bandana thrown to him by some noblewoman.:Assassin");
gladiadores.insert_last("Quintos:3001:4000:300000:Quintus is a rough and cruel man.  His enormous stature and build make this gladiator a truly terrifying figure.  Known for a great battle scar that begins on his forehead and ends near his chin, Quintus, the \"split-face\", as his barracks companions call him, is greatly respected.  To his companions it is a joking nickname.  To his opponents, the title of \"Destroyer\" is far more fitting.:Assassin");
gladiadores.insert_last("Marcus:4001:5000:330000:Marcus is a gladiator from the region of Germania.  He came as a slave, a trophy of battle for Julius Caesar, who at first thought of throwing him to the lions.  However, Caius Felix saw in his physique great potential for fighting in the arena.  Caius then asked the Emperor for permission to train him in the ludus, and Caesar backed him.  Marcus then went through intense training and gradually acquired a taste for spilled blood.  Today Marcus is an enormous gladiator with long blond hair, the same shade as his great beard.  He has a scar running diagonally across his torso and another on his head, marked by a large bald patch in his scalp.:War Developer");
gladiadores.insert_last("Barca:4001:5000:350000:After the Romans defeated the Carthaginians, the survivors were forced to fight one another in the arena.  Barca was the last survivor.  His final fight was against Mago, the chief of his people.  He was his father.  Barca is today a gladiator without any feeling, without any sense of kinship.  His only occupation is killing opponents so as not to think of his father's agonized face.:War Developer");
gladiadores.insert_last("Theokoles:5001:6000:400000:Theokoles, \"the shadow of death\", was a giant of a gladiator.  He stood over 3 meters tall.  He once fought 100 men.  He killed them all.  Before retiring he faced one last opponent who defeated him.  The name of that opponent, Doctore.:War Chief");
gladiadores.insert_last("Crixus:6001:7000:500000:Crixus is the finest gladiator in the ludus of Batiatus, the Champion of Capua.  Arrogant and powerful, he holds a deep dislike for Spartacus, whose rise threatens to upset his reign in the ludus.  Crixus goes his own way awaiting opponents, all while defending his place at the top of the gladiator hierarchy.:Commander");
gladiadores.insert_last("Tetraites:7001:8000:600000:Tetraites was originally discovered through graffiti found in Pompeii in 1817, depicting his courageous victory over Prudes.  Fighting in the murmillo style, he used a short sword, a large rectangular shield, a helmet, arm guards and greaves.  The full extent of Tetraites' fame was only understood in the late twentieth century, when pottery was found in places as distant as France and England bearing illustrations of the gladiator's victories.:Commander");
gladiadores.insert_last("Prisco:7001:8000:650000:The battle between Priscus and Verus in the first century AD was the first gladiator combat held in the famous Flavian Amphitheatre, the Colosseum of Rome.  After a fierce battle that dragged on for hours, the two gladiators yielded to one another at the same moment, lowering their swords in mutual respect.  The crowd, enthralled by the fight, roared its approval, and the Emperor Titus awarded them both the rudis, a small wooden sword given to gladiators upon their retirement.  Priscus and Verus left the arena as free men.:Convict of Disgrace");
gladiadores.insert_last("Spiculus:10000:12000:700000:Spiculus had a specially close relationship with the emperor Nero.  After countless victories by Spiculus, Nero granted him palaces, slaves and riches beyond imagination.  When Nero was deposed in 68 AD, he asked his aides to find Spiculus, because he wished to die at the hands of the famous gladiator.  But Spiculus could not be found, and so Nero was forced to take his own life.:Unreliable Madman");
gladiadores.insert_last("Marco AtÃ­lio:10000:12000:750000:Although he was a Roman citizen by birth, Atilius chose to enter the gladiator school in an attempt to clear the heavy debts he had run up during his life.  In his first battle he defeated Hilarus, a gladiator belonging to Nero who had already won thirteen times in a row.  In another bout, Atilius defeated Raecius Felix, who had won twelve battles in a row.  The deeds of Atilius were recorded in mosaics and graffiti discovered in 2007.:Unreliable Madman");
gladiadores.insert_last("Carpoforo:12001:16000:850000:While the other gladiators on this list are known for their hand-to-hand combat against other human beings, Carpophorus was a famous bestiarius.  These gladiators fought exclusively against wild animals and, as a result, had very short careers.  Fighting in the early days of the Flavian Amphitheatre, Carpophorus became famous for defeating a bear, a lion and a leopard in the same battle.  In another bout that same day, he killed a rhinoceros with a spear.  In all, it is said that he killed twenty wild animals in a single day, leading fans and fellow fighters alike to compare Carpophorus to the mythological Hercules.:Zombie");
gladiadores.insert_last("Flamma:12001:16000:900000:Flamma, a Syrian slave, died at the age of thirty, after fighting thirty-four times and winning twenty-one of those bouts. Nine battles ended in a draw, and he was defeated only four times. Most remarkable of all is that Flamma was awarded the rudis four times. When the rudis was given to a gladiator, he was freed and could live normally among Roman citizens. Flamma, however, refused his freedom, choosing to keep fighting in the arena.:Zombie");
gladiadores.insert_last("Spartacus:16001:20000:950000:Lentulus Batiatus of Capua must have recognized his potential, because he bought Spartacus with the intention of turning him into a gladiator.  But the fierce independence of a warrior is not easily tamed.  In 73 BC, Spartacus persuaded seventy of his fellow gladiators, Crixus among them, to rebel against their master.  In the revolt Batiatus was killed and the gladiators fled to the slopes of nearby Mount Vesuvius.  Betrayed, Spartacus is sold to the Romans as a slave and bought by a gladiator school.  But fate had greater plans for the Thracian soldier - he would become the leader of the greatest revolution of antiquity, and his name would remain forever engraved in history.  As they fled, the group gathered free men and many other slaves as well, thus becoming a large and powerful army.  The gladiators spent the winter of 72 BC training the newly freed slaves in preparation for what is now known as the Third Servile War, their ranks swelling to as many as 70,000 people.  Entire legions were sent to kill Spartacus, but they were easily defeated by the fighting spirit and experience of the gladiators.  In 71 BC, Marcus Licinius Crassus assembled 50,000 well-trained Roman soldiers to hunt down and defeat Spartacus.  Crassus cornered Spartacus in southern Italy, defeating his forces and killing the former slave (Spartacus' body, however, was never found).  Six thousand of Spartacus' followers were captured and crucified along the Appian Way, the road from Capua to Rome.:Monster");
